    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "4-Chloro-2-propylbenzenesulfonic acid has a benzene ring. C 1, C 2, and C 4 are bonded to sulfonic acid, a propyl group, and a chlorine atom, respectively."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "Meta-chlorobenzene reacts with sulfur trioxide in the presence of sulfuric acid to form 4-chloro-2-propylbenzenesulfonic acid. The same products cannot be formed from.o-Propylbenzenesulfonic acid or p-chlorobenzenesulfonic acid upon chlorination or alkylation, respectively."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00007",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00007-alt",
          "text": "Palladium-catalyzed hydrogenation of m-chloropropiophenone forms m-chloropropylbenzene."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_007.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00008",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00008-alt",
          "text": "Propiophenone reacts with chlorine in the presence of iron trichloride to form m-chloropropiophenone."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_008.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00009",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00009-alt",
          "text": "Benzene reacts with propanoyl chloride in the presence of aluminum trichloride to form propiophenone."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_009.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00010",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00198:alt:fig-00010-alt",
          "text": "A four-step reaction shows the formation of 4-chloro-2-propylbenzenesulfonic acid from benzene. It involves acylation then chlorination, then hydrogenation, and lastly sulfonation."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_10_010.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
